/* readings — is every row of this instrument log well formed?

   Each line of `examples/assets/readings.csv` carries the answer first, then a
   reading: a station, a date, and a temperature. The program judges the reading
   on its own and leaves with 1 if it ever disagrees with the answer.

       cufet examples/parsing/readings.cufe

   ── Why this program is in the corpus ──

   ★★ It was written to CHECK A PREDICTION recorded before it existed. Cufet has
   no named loops — no label letting `Stop.` or `Skip.` act on an outer loop —
   and the trigger for adding them was "the first program that needs a mutable
   flag purely to break an outer loop". The prediction said that witness would
   be a `Skip.` rather than a `Stop.` — CONTINUE the outer loop — in a program
   that scans, and that a CSV reader was the likeliest shape, because the four
   `break`s in the C program this corpus borrowed its beamforming data from all
   live in its CSV reader.

   ⚠ It was written the way it would have been written anyway. Bending a program
   toward the answer you predicted is how you learn nothing.

   The exit contract:

       0   the reader agreed with every recorded answer
       1   it disagreed about at least one row
       2   the file could not be read

   ── What the shape turned out to be ──

   ★★ THERE IS NO NESTED LOOP HERE AT ALL, and that is the finding. A row is
   judged by three checks that do not resemble each other, so nothing loops over
   the fields; and the character-level scanning that WOULD have looped — is this
   a month, is this a decimal, is this a station code — is what a pattern is for.
   `regex` absorbed the inner loop before it could exist.

   ★ So the prediction's *shape* was right and its *witness* was wrong: a
   continue-outer wants a nested scan, and in a language with patterns the
   nested scan does not get written. See DESIGN, *Named loops stay out, and
   patterns are why*, which now records both.
*/

Pull a book on regex.

    /* A station code: letters, a dash, a number. */
    Define regex station as [^[A-Z]+-\d+$].

    /* A date that is real enough to trust — the month and day ranges are in the
       pattern rather than in a check afterwards, which is most of why no loop
       appears below. ⚠ It does not know how long February is; nothing here
       claims to. */
    Define regex stamp as [^(19|20)\d\d-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\d|3[01])$].

    /* A temperature: whole or decimal, possibly below zero. */
    Define regex reading as [^-?\d+(\.\d+)?$].

    /// Is this row a well-formed reading?
    ///
    /// ★ Three checks, not a loop over three fields — they share no rule. The
    /// count is checked first because the others would read past the end.
    Bind fact to sound, given (the series of text fields):
        If the number of fields is not 4, return false.
        If cast station on (item 2 of fields) is false, return false.
        If cast stamp on (item 3 of fields) is false, return false.
        If cast reading on (item 4 of fields) is false, return false.
        Return true.
    Done.

    Bind void to usage:
        State "usage: readings".
        State "".
        State "judges every row in examples/assets/readings.csv against the answer it carries".
        State "leaves with 0 if they all agreed, 1 if any disagreed, 2 on a bad file".
    Done.

    Bind void to play:
        Define target as "examples/assets/readings.csv".

        If the number of the arguments is greater than 0:
            Cast usage.
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        Define lines as a series of text.
        Try to:
            The lines becomes read all lines from the file target.
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            State "readings: {target}: {the message of the failure}".
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        Define checked as 0.
        Define disagreements as 0.

        For each line in lines, repeat:
            If the length of line is 0, skip.

            Define fields as line split by ",".
            Define expected as item 1 of fields.
            Define ours as cast sound on (fields).
            Define theirs as expected is "ok".
            Increment checked by 1.

            Define mark as "  ".
            If ours is not theirs:
                The mark becomes "!!".
                Increment disagreements by 1.
            Done.

            State "{mark} {line}: we say {ours converted to text}, the file says {theirs converted to text}".
        Done.

        State "".
        State "{checked converted to text} row(s) checked".
        State "{disagreements converted to text} disagreement(s)".

        If disagreements is greater than 0, exit with 1.
        Exit.
    Done.

    Cast play.
Done.
